<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<chapter xml:id="grv-ch-birational">
  <title>Blowups and stable birational geometry</title>
  <introduction><p>For a blowup of a smooth variety along a smooth center, the bundle calculations express the change of class as a multiple of <m>\LL</m>. To make this observation useful for arbitrary varieties, one needs a presentation that controls all relations among smooth projective models. Bittner's theorem supplies that presentation; the quotient by <m>\LL</m> then becomes a precise stable birational invariant. We use the quotient both to distinguish geometric classes and to study the size of the ring. After inverting <m>\LL</m>, the same presentation gives a duality involution.</p></introduction>
  <section xml:id="grv-sec-bittner">
    <title>Blowups and projective generators</title>
    <p>The scissors presentation is well suited to decompositions into affine pieces. A different presentation makes smooth projective varieties available as generators and replaces arbitrary cutting by a controlled geometric operation. Throughout this chapter, <m>k</m> is algebraically closed of characteristic zero.</p>
    <proposition xml:id="grv-prop-blowup">
      <title>The blowup formula</title>
      <statement><p>Let <m>X</m> be smooth, and let <m>Z\subset X</m> be a smooth closed subvariety of pure codimension <m>c\ge1</m>. Then
        <md number="no">[\Bl_ZX]=[X]+(\LL+\cdots+\LL^{c-1})[Z].</md>
        For <m>c=1</m> the parenthesized sum is zero.</p></statement>
      <proof><p>The blowup is an isomorphism away from <m>Z</m>. Its exceptional divisor is <m>E=\PP(N_{Z/X})</m>, a projective bundle of relative dimension <m>c-1</m>. Thus
        <md number="no">[\Bl_ZX]-[X]=[E]-[Z]=([\PP^{c-1}]-1)[Z].</md>
        The projective bundle formula gives the assertion. Centers with components of different codimensions are handled component by component.</p></proof>
    </proposition>
    <theorem xml:id="grv-thm-bittner">
      <title>Bittner's presentation</title>
      <statement><p>The group underlying <m>\Rk</m> is generated by isomorphism classes of smooth projective <m>k</m>-varieties, subject to the empty-variety relation, disjoint-union additivity, and the relations
        <md number="no">[\Bl_ZX]-[E]=[X]-[Z]</md>
        for smooth projective <m>X</m> and smooth closed centers <m>Z</m>, where <m>E</m> is the exceptional divisor. Products of generators give the usual ring structure.</p></statement>
    </theorem>
    <p>This is <xref ref="grv-bib-Bittner" detail="Theorem 3.1"/>. Its proof uses resolution of singularities and weak factorization, which are external inputs here. Smooth stratification and compactification produce the projective generators. To see the resulting expression, consider a smooth variety <m>U</m> with a simple normal crossings compactification.</p>
    <p>For example, if <m>U</m> is smooth and has a smooth projective compactification <m>\overline U</m> with a simple normal crossings boundary <m>D=\bigcup_{i=1}^rD_i</m>, repeated scissors relations give
      <md number="no">[U]=\sum_{I\subseteq\{1,\ldots,r\}}(-1)^{|I|}[D_I],\qquad D_\varnothing=\overline U,\quad D_I=\bigcap_{i\in I}D_i.</md>
      The terms are smooth projective, with disconnected intersections interpreted additively. Weak factorization compares any two such choices through smooth blowups and proves independence using precisely the displayed relations. This is the additional step that identifies all the relations among the generators.</p>
    <p xml:id="grv-anchor-bittner-scope">Consequently, an additive invariant of smooth projective varieties extends to an additive invariant of all varieties once it respects the blowup relation. If it is also compatible with products, the extension is a ring homomorphism. This is the construction used by the Hodge polynomial and by the motivic Euler characteristic studied in <xref ref="grv-ch-motives"/>.</p>
    <p xml:id="margin-grv-bittner-scope">This also answers the presentation question from <xref ref="grv-rem-object-conventions" text="custom">the object conventions</xref>: smooth projective generators with the blowup relations recover the characteristic-zero ring. Disjoint-union relations alone would not encode the comparisons between different projective models.</p>
  </section>
  <section xml:id="grv-sec-stable-birational">
    <title>Quotient by the affine line</title>
    <definition xml:id="grv-def-stable-birational">
      <statement><p>Integral varieties <m>X,Y</m> are <term>stably birational</term> if <m>X\times\PP^a</m> and <m>Y\times\PP^b</m> are birational for some <m>a,b\ge0</m>. Let <m>\SB_k</m> be the commutative monoid of stable birational classes of smooth projective integral varieties, with product induced by Cartesian product. Write <m>\Z[\SB_k]</m> for its monoid ring.</p></statement>
    </definition>
    <p>As an abelian group, <m>\Z[\SB_k]</m> is free on these classes. The multiplicative monoid need not have cancellation: an isomorphism between two products need not allow a common factor to be removed. We will see this through abelian varieties.</p>
    <theorem xml:id="grv-thm-larsen-lunts">
      <title>Larsen–Lunts</title>
      <statement><p>There is an isomorphism of rings
        <md number="no">\Rk/(\LL)\ \simeq\ \Z[\SB_k]</md>
        taking the class of each smooth projective integral variety to its stable birational class.</p></statement>
      <proof><p>First assign to a smooth projective variety the sum of the stable birational classes of its connected components. For a smooth center of positive codimension, the blowup is birational to the original variety, while the exceptional divisor is stably birational to the center. If the center contains an entire connected component, both the blowup of that component and its exceptional divisor are empty, and its contribution to the relation is <m>0=[Z]-[Z]</m>. Disjoint-union additivity reduces to these two cases. The assignment respects every blowup relation, so Bittner's theorem extends it to a ring homomorphism. Both <m>\PP^1</m> and a point have the same stable birational class, so <m>\LL=[\PP^1]-1</m> maps to zero.</p>
        <p>Conversely, send a stable birational class with smooth projective representative <m>X</m> to <m>[X]\bmod\LL</m>. Weak factorization and the blowup formula show that smooth projective birational varieties have the same class modulo <m>\LL</m>. Moreover <m>[X\times\PP^a]\equiv[X]\pmod{\LL}</m>. Thus the assignment is independent of the representative. The two maps are inverse on the smooth projective generators.</p></proof>
    </theorem>
    <p>The original construction appears in <xref ref="grv-bib-LL03" detail="Section 2"/>; the characteristic-zero field formulation is also explained in <xref ref="grv-bib-Poonen" detail="Section 4"/>. The proof above uses weak factorization as an external theorem, in addition to Bittner's presentation.</p>
    <corollary xml:id="grv-cor-sb-test">
      <statement><p>For smooth projective integral <m>X,Y</m>, one has
        <md number="no">[X]-[Y]\in(\LL)\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad X\text{ and }Y\text{ are stably birational}.</md>
        In particular, <m>[X]\equiv1\pmod{\LL}</m> if and only if <m>X</m> is stably rational.</p></statement>
      <proof><p>Distinct elements of <m>\SB_k</m> are distinct basis elements of the free abelian group <m>\Z[\SB_k]</m>.</p></proof>
    </corollary>
    <p>The projectivity and smoothness in this criterion are substantive. For example, <m>[\A^1]=0</m> modulo <m>\LL</m>, although its function field is rational. Applying the stable birational class of an arbitrary open variety directly to its class in the quotient gives the wrong answer.</p>
    <p xml:id="grv-ex-birational-not-equal">The blowup calculation shows exactly what the quotient discards. The surfaces <m>\Bl_p\PP^2</m> and <m>\PP^2</m> are birational, but their classes differ by <m>\LL\ne0</m>. Likewise <m>\PP^1</m> and a point are stably birational and have classes differing by <m>\LL</m>. Both pairs become equal modulo <m>\LL</m>, although their classes in the original ring are distinct.</p>
    <p xml:id="grv-anchor-mod-vs-invert">Reduction modulo <m>\LL</m> retains this stable birational information by setting the affine line equal to zero. The zero-divisor constructions in <xref ref="grv-ch-zero-divisors"/> will instead produce nonzero differences that disappear when <m>\LL</m> is inverted. The two operations have different purposes.</p>
    <p xml:id="margin-grv-mod-vs-invert">Indeed, a unital realization that sends <m>\LL</m> to zero cannot extend to the localization with a nonzero target: it would have to send a unit to zero. We will use the stable birational quotient to prove nonvanishing before localization, and other realizations to test what survives it.</p>
  </section>
  <section xml:id="grv-sec-birational-tests">
    <title>Stable birational tests and the size of the ring</title>
    <p>In practice, a product relation supplies a possible zero divisor, and stable birational geometry certifies that its factors are nonzero. For abelian varieties, the Albanese variety makes this particularly effective.</p>
    <proposition xml:id="grv-prop-abelian-sb">
      <statement><p>Two abelian varieties over <m>k</m> are stably birational if and only if they are isomorphic as varieties. Consequently, nonisomorphic abelian varieties <m>A,B</m> satisfy <m>[A]-[B]\notin(\LL)</m>.</p></statement>
      <proof><p>For smooth projective varieties the Albanese variety is a birational invariant, and <m>\Alb(X\times\PP^r)\simeq\Alb(X)</m>. These standard properties follow from the universal property of the Albanese variety and extension of rational maps to abelian varieties. Since <m>\Alb(A)=A</m> as an abelian variety after choosing an origin, stable birationality implies that the abelian varieties are isomorphic. The converse is immediate, and the last assertion follows from <xref ref="grv-cor-sb-test"/>.</p></proof>
    </proposition>
    <p>For smooth projective varieties that are both non-uniruled, stable birationality also implies birationality. This is a consequence of the birational invariance and product behavior of the maximal rationally connected quotient, used here as an external theorem. It applies to the Calabi–Yau centers in <xref ref="grv-thm-imou"/>; see <xref ref="grv-bib-IMOU" detail="proof of Theorem 1.3"/>. The non-uniruled hypothesis supplies the birational conclusion and excludes applying the argument to arbitrary rationally connected varieties.</p>
    <p>The same quotient detects more than a single nonzero difference. Because its additive group is free on stable birational classes, geometric restrictions on products of those classes can exhibit ideals that are not finitely generated.</p>
    <theorem xml:id="grv-thm-nonnoetherian">
      <title>A large stable birational quotient</title>
      <statement><p>The ring <m>\Rk</m> is not Noetherian.</p></statement>
    </theorem>
    <p>This is <xref ref="grv-bib-LiuSebag" detail="Corollary 4"/>. Their proof works in <m>\Z[\SB_k]</m>: a sequence of smooth projective curves of suitably increasing genera yields an ideal that is not finitely generated. The geometric input rules out expressing a new curve class as the product of an earlier curve class with another stable birational class. Since a quotient of a Noetherian ring is Noetherian, the result for the quotient implies the result for <m>\Rk</m>. This argument uses the geometry of the monoid, not merely the existence of infinitely many generators.</p>
    <p>For another indication of size, <xref ref="grv-bib-LiuSebag" detail="Corollary 3"/> proves algebraic independence for the classes of pairwise nonisogenous simple abelian varieties. Here the Albanese variety distinguishes products: an isomorphism of products preserves the multiplicities of their simple isogeny factors. Thus distinct monomials give distinct stable birational basis elements. In particular, the ring contains many algebraically independent geometric classes, while their multiplication in the ambient ring can still have nontrivial annihilators.</p>
    <p>The free stable birational quotient used here should be compared with the dimension-truncated groups in <xref ref="grv-app-comparisons"/>. That appendix proves the leading-birational-class description in a fixed dimension range and explains why higher-dimensional relations prevent the same description of the full ring.</p>
  </section>
  <section xml:id="grv-sec-duality">
    <title>Duality after localization</title>
    <p>The blowup presentation also allows us to normalize the class of a smooth projective variety by its dimension. The normalization involves negative powers of <m>\LL</m>, so its target is the localized ring <m>\Mk=\Rk[\LL^{-1}]</m>.</p>
    <theorem xml:id="grv-thm-bittner-duality">
      <title>Bittner's duality involution</title>
      <idx>Bittner duality</idx><idx>duality involution</idx>
      <statement><p>There is a unique ring involution <m>\mathcal D:\Mk\to\Mk</m> such that, for every smooth connected projective variety <m>X</m> of dimension <m>d</m>,
        <md number="no">\mathcal D([X])=\LL^{-d}[X].</md>
        It satisfies <m>\mathcal D(\LL)=\LL^{-1}</m> and <m>\mathcal D^2=\operatorname{id}</m>.</p></statement>
      <proof><p>Assign <m>\delta([X])=\LL^{-\dim X}[X]</m> to each smooth connected projective generator. We check the blowup relation. Let <m>Z\subset X</m> be a smooth connected center of codimension <m>c\geq1</m>, and let <m>E</m> be the exceptional divisor. Since <m>[E]=(1+\LL+\cdots+\LL^{c-1})[Z]</m>,
        <md number="no">(1-\LL)[E]=(1-\LL^c)[Z].</md>
        Adding this to <m>[\Bl_ZX]-[E]=[X]-[Z]</m> gives
        <md number="no">[\Bl_ZX]-\LL[E]=[X]-\LL^c[Z].</md>
        Dividing by <m>\LL^d</m>, where <m>d=\dim X</m>, proves
        <md number="no">\delta([\Bl_ZX])-\delta([E])=\delta([X])-\delta([Z]),</md>
        because <m>\dim E=d-1</m> and <m>\dim Z=d-c</m>. Disjoint unions reduce general centers to their components; a center equal to a component of <m>X</m> gives the identity <m>0=\delta([Z])-\delta([Z])</m>. Bittner's presentation therefore defines an additive map <m>\delta:\Rk\to\Mk</m>.</p>
        <p>Dimensions add under products, so <m>\delta</m> is multiplicative on the projective generators, hence on <m>\Rk</m>. It preserves the unit, and
          <md number="no">\delta(\LL)=\delta([\PP^1]-1)=\LL^{-1}[\PP^1]-1=\LL^{-1}.</md>
          Its value on <m>\LL</m> is a unit, so localization extends it uniquely to a ring endomorphism <m>\mathcal D</m> of <m>\Mk</m>. On a projective generator,
          <md number="no">\mathcal D^2([X])=\mathcal D(\LL^{-d}[X])=\LL^d\LL^{-d}[X]=[X].</md>
          The square also fixes <m>\LL^{-1}</m>. These elements generate <m>\Mk</m>, proving the involution property and uniqueness. This is the argument of <xref ref="grv-bib-Bittner" detail="Corollary 3.4 and Lemma 3.5"/>.</p>
      </proof>
    </theorem>
    <p>For projective space, <m>\mathcal D([\PP^n])=1+\LL^{-1}+\cdots+\LL^{-n}</m>. For the nonproper variety <m>\mathbb G_m</m>, additivity instead gives
      <md number="no">\mathcal D([\mathbb G_m])=\LL^{-1}-1=-\LL^{-1}[\mathbb G_m].</md>
      Thus the dimension-normalized formula on smooth projective generators determines the dual of an open variety through its scissors class.</p>
    <p>The virtual Hodge polynomial extends to <m>E_k:\Mk\to\Z[u^{\pm1},v^{\pm1}]</m>. Poincaré duality on smooth projective generators gives
      <md number="no">E_k(\mathcal D(a);u,v)=E_k(a;u^{-1},v^{-1})\qquad(a\in\Mk).</md>
      Indeed, on a smooth projective <m>d</m>-fold the right side is <m>(uv)^{-d}E_k(X;u,v)</m>, and the two homomorphisms agree on these generators and on <m>\LL^{-1}</m>. The corresponding duality of Chow motives is described in <xref ref="grm-duality" text="custom">the motivic comparison</xref>.</p>
  </section>
</chapter>
